package ${package}

import io.koraframework.json.common.annotation.Json
import io.koraframework.json.common.annotation.JsonDiscriminatorField
${imports}

/**
 * Sealed hierarchy for ${entity_name} with polymorphic JSON.
 *
 * The "${discriminator_field}" property selects the concrete subtype on read and is written
 * automatically by the generated mapper — subtypes do **not** need a property for it. The
 * reader buffers the object, so the discriminator may appear anywhere in the document.
 *
 * Every subtype needs its own `@Json` (or `@JsonReader` / `@JsonWriter`); the sealed mapper
 * is composed from the per-subtype mappers. See `sealed-dto-impl.kt.template`.
 *
 * JSON example:
 * ```
 * ${json_example}
 * ```
 */
@Json
@JsonDiscriminatorField("${discriminator_field}")
sealed interface ${sealed_interface_name}

/*
 * Options:
 *
 *   @JsonDiscriminatorField(value = "${discriminator_field}", defaultValue = "…")
 *       subtype used when the document carries no discriminator at all; without it such a
 *       document fails to read.
 *
 *   @JsonReader / @JsonWriter in place of @Json
 *       generate only one direction for the whole hierarchy.
 *
 * Nesting the subtypes inside this interface keeps the permitted set in one file, which is
 * the shape the Kora examples use.
 */
